✅ QUESTION 1: MINIMUM NUMBER OF JUMPS TO REACH END (Jump Game II)
🔹 Problem Statement

You are given an integer array nums, where each element represents the maximum number of steps you can jump forward from that index.

Your task is to return the minimum number of jumps required to reach the last index of the array.

📌 You can assume that it is always possible to reach the last index.

✏️ Example
Input:  nums = [2, 3, 1, 1, 4]
Output: 2
Explanation:
Jump from index 0 → index 1 → index 4

🔹 Approach Used: Greedy Algorithm
💡 Key Idea

Instead of checking all possible jumps (which is slow), we:

Always jump to the farthest reachable position

Increment jump count only when necessary

🔹 Variables Used
Variable	Purpose
jumps	Counts minimum jumps
currentEnd	End of range reachable with current jumps
farthest	Farthest index reachable so far
🔹 Algorithm (Step-by-Step)

Start from index 0

For each index:

Calculate the farthest reachable position

When you reach the boundary (currentEnd):

Increase jump count

Move boundary to farthest

Repeat until second last index

⏱ Time Complexity: O(n)
📦 Space Complexity: O(1)

🔹 Java Code
class Solution {
    public int jump(int[] nums) {
        int jumps = 0;
        int currentEnd = 0;
        int farthest = 0;

        // Traverse till second last index
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {

            // Calculate the farthest index reachable
            farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i]);

            // If we reached the end of current jump range
            if (i == currentEnd) {
                jumps++;
                currentEnd = farthest;
            }
        }
        return jumps;
    }
}

